:: Day 3 KEB

Devna bounced on her toes, resettling the bag on her back and tightening the straps just a nudge. After more then three years of theory and finetuning technology and training, they were finally ready. She was standing in the room with the portal frame - a permanent installation that finetuned the portal crafting that would allow them to travel beyond their own world and act as an anchor to allow them to return.

Myor joined her, fidgeting with his own backpack straps then checking on the various tools attached to his belt. He pulled out the Home Anchor, a small cylinder that fit easily into his smaller hands, checking it over carefully before putting it back away. It was durable, Devna knew because she had spent nearly eleven months redesigning it to ensure it wouldn't accidentally break and strand Myor if anything forced them to split up.

The fact her partner was a Ratfolk was one of the better outcomes Devna hoped for. Not just because he was still shorter than Devna, even if he was on the taller side of the Ratfolk at just over five feet, but because Ratfolk were one of the more adaptable peoples. While they preferred a vegetarian diet, they could eat almost anything. They were wily, flexible in thinking, and social. Besides, Devna had bonded best with Myor during the initial training to select the final candidates.

There had been concerns that Ratfolk and Humans strengths were too similar which meant they weren't covering for their weaknesses, but that's not how Devna thought about it. Her and Myor got along, were both curious and willing to challenge the unknown, and would prefer finding a solution rather than never fix something looking for the "perfect" solution.

"Mr. Ackeray, confirm the Home Anchor is secured on your person," Jackson called, entering the secured room. A heavyset elf, he dressed impeccably and was proud to lead this expedition. As much shit as Devna liked to give him, they'd never have made it this far if Jackson hadn't pushed so hard for it.

"Yes yes," Myor said, patting the belt pouch the Home Anchor was in. "Secured."

"Ms. Sorrel, confirm the Wayfarer Sigil," there was a slight flinch at the name, " is secured on your person."

Devna grinned, patting her own belt pouch where a very similar cylinder to the Home Anchor was located. Both items served a similar function, though through different means, and she had successfully argued they should have different names. Jackson was just sore that her and Myor overruled him in naming it.

"For the record," Jackson sighed.

"Yup," Devna said, "Wayfarer Sigil is secured and prepared for use on my person."

Jackson nodded, turning to the empty doorway frame in the middle of the room. "Once the portal is stabilized, Mr. Ackeray and Ms. Sorrel will enter the portal. We will leave it open for five minutes to provide the opportunity for your immediate return if for any reason the other side is inhospitable. Once through, the rest of the mission is entirely at your discretion." He nodded towards the control room on the other side of protective glass before turning back to Myor and Devna, "Your top priority is for both of you to return. Secondary goals include sample collection, magic experimentation, and contact with civilization, if one so happens to exist. Any questions?"

They both stayed silent, Myor's eyes focused on the soft glow starting to concentrate within the empty doorway.

"In which case," Jackson paused, turning to watch as the soft glow condensed into a shimmery, hazy effect, "safe travels."

Myor finally looked to Devon, whiskers twitching in excitement. Devna nodded, "Let's go then."

Devna led the way. During training they had decided it made most since for Devna to go first. Being human, if there was something wacky going on with magic she wouldn't be paralyzed by it. There had been arguments that if something magic immediately attacked them, she'd be at a disadvantage, but Devna felt pretty confident in her Arcane Ward. She'd built it as part of her thesis nearly a decade ago and through tinkering had improved it over the years. It was a small device shaped to be pretty enough to wear as a necklace, but the important part was it weakened and reduced all magical effects directed at her.

Reaching the threshold she didn't pause. She walked through with her hand at her Arcane Ward, ready to flick it on once through.

They'd opened several portals over the last three months, but this would be the first one anyone actually traveled through. Her immediate impressions were underwhelming. It felt like walking through a doorway into a dark room.

Then Devna tripped over something and fell face first into bark. Luckily, it was a large fallen log, tall enough she couldn't see over it. Immediately, Devna shifted to the left and tried to look around.

It wasn't just the log in front of her, glancing up it looked like it was night cycle, with faint impressions of canopy high above. Everything around her was blocked by thick undergrowth and there was a strong earthy scent. The air was sticky with humidity and lack of wind. Her stumbling entrance had likely spooked the wildlife, but insects were buzzing away and the occasional animal call rang out.

Myor appeared and stepped forward, also getting caught by whatever was at their feet. Devna helped brace him, saving him from getting close and personal with the log, and gave him a quick check. Visually he didn't seem bothered by the portal any more than she had been.

"You good?" Devna asked to confirm.

Myor looked around, nose twitching. "Hmm, physically yes. The portal itself was nothing. But the magic here is… heavy."

"Heavy?" Devna asked, everyone seemed to prefer describing magic differently, but most commonly settled into threads or river analogies. Myor himself tended to prefer water related descriptions. "Like being at the bottom of a deep lake?"

"No no, like syrup," Myor corrected. "Maybe I need time to adjust. I don't think we need an immediate return, do you?"

Devna looked around and shook her head. "Let me give you a boost up to see over this log, then you can help haul me up. Give us a better vantage to see what's around."

The packs were irritating, but that's what training had been for. It took only a moment or so for Devna to brace then provide a boost to Myor. He clambered up and reached down a hand to help haul her up. Thankfully the wood helped with that, providing easy footholds and grips to climb it.

They both sat, looking out at the darkness. "I think we're above the undergrowth?" Devna ventured. Height hadn't made a difference to the lack of light.

Myor started muttering under his breath, hands flexing almost like a cat kneading. Devna watched him bemused, he was a silent caster. It was a point of pride that he was skilled enough to not need vocal or gestures to aid his control. Slowly, with Myor sounding more and more strained, light started gathering until a small orb bobbed gently around Myor until it settled just behind him and off to the right a bit.

It was gentle, but enough to let them see the forest around them. The trees were giant, stretching far above with the lowermost branches only just barely within the light spell. Undergrowth was thick, making seeing anything on the ground difficult to see.

Nothing attacked the light, which was good in Devna's book. "A pain to walk through," Devna pointed out, "But looking good so far. You've successfully cast a spell, let me check my tech."

Myor nodded, gazing out at the forest.

Devna was just running a diagnostic when Myor started patting her arm in excitement, "A road! Look, that's a road!"

Devna turned to follow his pointing. Off on his other side, the undergrowth made way for dirt path. It wasn't paved, but even from this distance it was clear the undergrowth had been deliberately cleared and was likely maintained.

A gentle buzz in her hand brought her attention back down, the diagnostic came back clear. Her tech was running strong and had a strong indication of magic in the surrounding.

"Well, you've got magic working, my tech seems solid, there's sign of civilization and nothing has tried to kill us," Devna summarized.

"I vote follow the road," Myor cut in immediately, eyes darting around again. "Your vote?"

[[Follow the road->Unwritten]]
[[Collect sample and return home->Unwritten]]
